#	/etc/mail/virtuals — mail guest
#
#	Referenced from smtpd.conf as:  table virtuals file:/etc/mail/virtuals
#	and used by the inbound action ("virtual <virtuals>").
#
#	Format (table(5), "virtual" mapping): left-hand side is the address mail
#	was sent to, right-hand side is the local address it becomes.  Values are
#	expanded before delivery, so a value that is itself an alias in
#	/etc/mail/aliases will be expanded again by that table.
#
#	Reload after editing:  rcctl reload smtpd
#	Test by sending a message to the mapped address and watching
#	/var/log/maillog on this guest.

# --- catch-all -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything sent to the domain reaches the admin mailbox unless a more
# specific line below says otherwise.  Order in a file-backed table does not
# matter; smtpd picks the most specific match.
@lab.example.org	admin@lab.example.org

# --- role addresses -------------------------------------------------------------
# These are the addresses that appear in the DNS zone (SPF, DMARC, SOA) and in
# the CAA record's iodef, so they must exist.
postmaster@lab.example.org	admin@lab.example.org
hostmaster@lab.example.org	admin@lab.example.org
dmarc@lab.example.org		admin@lab.example.org
abuse@lab.example.org		admin@lab.example.org
security@lab.example.org	admin@lab.example.org

# --- people ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# One line per human.  The right-hand side is a real mailbox on this host
# (dovecot's userdb makes the directory exist on first login).
admin@lab.example.org		admin@lab.example.org

# --- notes -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# * Do not map an address to a system account name (/etc/passwd).  Everything
#   here is a *virtual* mailbox under /var/vmail; that separation is what lets
#   you rebuild a user without touching the system.
# * If you add a second domain later, keep it in a separate table file and pass
#   both to the action: "virtual <virtuals> virtual <virtuals-other>".
